#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091999 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c96e6d6f-22be-5bfd-b24e-be9e34fcb149" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "d2e0c6d3-e37e-5c12-be20-3d645b7c3b59" :question/math-academy-id "e-5359" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(x+2)^2}{18} - \\dfrac{(y+7)^2}{32} = 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k) = (-2,-7),\\qquad a=\\sqrt{18} = 3\\sqrt{2}, \\qquad b=\\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+7 & =\\pm (\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}})(x+2) \\\\ y+7 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3}(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092071 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56abbbc0-080d-5586-931f-957bd2b793b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "2d3b2c00-06c8-5fc8-b615-b5e1b7cd7b8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7428" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $9x^2 - y^2 - 36 x - 2y + 26 = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-36x-2y+26 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-36x-{y}^{2}-2y+26 & =0 \\\\ 9[{x}^{2}-4x]-[{y}^{2}+2y]+26 & =0 \\\\ 9[(x-2)^{2}-4]-[(y+1)^{2}-1]+26 & =0 \\\\ 9(x-2)^{2}-36-(y+1)^{2}+1+26 & =0 \\\\ 9(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =-26-1+36 \\\\ 9(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of the horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k = \\pm\\dfrac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nh = 2, \\qquad k = -1, \\qquad a = \\sqrt{1} = 1,\\qquad b = \\sqrt{9} = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\pm 3(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092072 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ace3c4b0-6b1c-595d-bc60-b89e2b24f6b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "3d9ae1ea-3b06-5167-b39a-3dc7ea09d8c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7430" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(y - 1) ^ 2} {16} - \\dfrac{(x + 3)^2}{9} = 1$." :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k = \\pm\\dfrac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{16} = 4, \\qquad b = \\sqrt{9} = 3, \\qquad h = -3, \\qquad k = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3}(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092073 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d19a514c-faa5-5f69-9870-87f78878f08a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "1e0f4c0f-ed23-5d76-a63c-77a65be647cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-7431" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $y^2 - x^2 - 2y = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y)-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}-1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}-{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of the vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}(x-h) .\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k) = (0,1), \\qquad a = \\sqrt{1} = 1, \\qquad b= \\sqrt{1} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\pm x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ace3c4b0-6b1c-595d-bc60-b89e2b24f6b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac20d2f9-ef95-549c-b8c7-5cfede04d451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=\\pm \\frac{9}{5}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340713} {:answer/id #uuid "78bf2910-a9ae-5e50-991c-5a54af2936b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340714} {:answer/id #uuid "669af26c-1bb6-581c-b58e-98a8b068fc78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=\\pm \\frac{5}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340715} {:answer/id #uuid "75b91d3f-45c6-52bf-b9b4-f7ceb749cb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340716} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a46bf0-b247-5213-bb1d-f892bae592c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{5}{3}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669af26c-1bb6-581c-b58e-98a8b068fc78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=\\pm \\frac{5}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340715} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f21e628-4d04-5de9-afab-10e1fce64c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df144c9c-31f2-594e-958f-a4bf1a0938ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-10470" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{25}=5,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,h=2,\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny-2=\\pm \\frac{5}{3}(x-2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c96e6d6f-22be-5bfd-b24e-be9e34fcb149" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc55c75-4a14-5a96-8c62-06a7180f3d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340720} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7b4798-2611-53ef-8ecf-dc51a386f607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{4}{9}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340721} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d2f8a6-4d2e-576b-bdfc-716e0a2bbc1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340722} {:answer/id #uuid "76a857f0-bae7-5db8-b393-e48bb0b1a35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340723} {:answer/id #uuid "6e55c50f-cdf6-5679-98f3-8c3e0d78fa2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{4}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5d2f8a6-4d2e-576b-bdfc-716e0a2bbc1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340722} :answer-field/id #uuid "7154fb86-2846-5dcc-893e-3e1c9489122d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edd95fbb-4a79-518d-bddc-9a9755b1164d" :question/math-academy-id "q-10472" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny+2=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56abbbc0-080d-5586-931f-957bd2b793b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "915e809a-b0b7-5c3b-9b7a-2eee711f1e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340727} {:answer/id #uuid "fb378d5f-1c54-566b-99c7-6903a656166d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340728} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8a242d-c825-5a86-b1a0-8c0f9fd9f17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340729} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3bd40f-657e-570e-9f5c-13725ca4272b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340730} {:answer/id #uuid "c57a1b68-82ae-51e7-893c-536419d5cac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c57a1b68-82ae-51e7-893c-536419d5cac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340731} :answer-field/id #uuid "92edd747-4141-5a9c-b88e-174903fb9281" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67b95450-22b9-5cce-8735-0135006f36b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-10476" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-32x+18y+19=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-32x+18y+19 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-32x-9{y}^{2}+18y+19 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-8x]-9[{y}^{2}-2y]+19 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-4)^{2}-16]-9[(y-1)^{2}-1]+19 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-4)^{2}-64-9(y-1)^{2}+9+19 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-4)^{2}-9(y-1)^{2} & =64-9-19 \\\\ 4(x-4)^{2}-9(y-1)^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(4,1),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny-1=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x-4).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d19a514c-faa5-5f69-9870-87f78878f08a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e7c577-a3a2-51e3-9de3-1caa802defc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340734} {:answer/id #uuid "4f815a4e-929e-5a91-927f-ed6de5e29500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-6=\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340735} {:answer/id #uuid "458cd9d3-bdc0-502d-9627-7c858fd98dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-6=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340736} {:answer/id #uuid "783cee4d-656a-5172-8c5c-ff16484c4383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-6=\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340737} {:answer/id #uuid "0425ee5b-4d95-5db7-9c86-fbf33b8aa090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "458cd9d3-bdc0-502d-9627-7c858fd98dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-6=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340736} :answer-field/id #uuid "d182e9c6-e0ba-5599-9493-2b77b938f789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77851a06-c0ae-5922-bbf6-af708ba25be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-10480" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-48y-10x+59=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-48y-10x+59 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-48y-5{x}^{2}-10x+59 & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-12y]-5[{x}^{2}+2x]+59 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y-6)^{2}-36]-5[(x+1)^{2}-1]+59 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-6)^{2}-144-5(x+1)^{2}+5+59 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-6)^{2}-5(x+1)^{2} & =144-5-59 \\\\ 4(y-6)^{2}-5(x+1)^{2} & =80 \\\\ \\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{20}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(-1,6),\\,a=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5},\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-6=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(x+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ace3c4b0-6b1c-595d-bc60-b89e2b24f6b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a95a2c9-522a-5d86-88c0-e5ff99128e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340741} {:answer/id #uuid "20ad743f-19d4-5118-91d4-d3a23cc46d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\sqrt{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340742} {:answer/id #uuid "2420da5d-3482-55be-9b8c-05dae6246b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340743} {:answer/id #uuid "336fa1b0-6e83-5ca5-a641-032a4b6ff0eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\sqrt{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340744} {:answer/id #uuid "0158a5c6-30eb-5e22-a988-f9675073ff3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a95a2c9-522a-5d86-88c0-e5ff99128e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340741} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a20373-9e89-5ac6-8f6d-3f0c4042efc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c56c9dd5-76a5-553a-ab03-9c4e5fe7c26a" :question/math-academy-id "q-16436" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{2},\\,h=-1,\\,k=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1) & =\\pm (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}})(x-(-1)) \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56abbbc0-080d-5586-931f-957bd2b793b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "959440f7-7db2-52e9-9617-b028063ff3ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 4 = ± \\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089605} {:answer/id #uuid "98bc9fd4-759d-5f8f-917c-56b143c4cdda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = ± 3(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089606} {:answer/id #uuid "d041be2b-8164-55be-96b7-48c878a7e289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = ± (x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089607} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb677a8-888a-5543-a6d1-529b862e9b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 4 = ± x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089608} {:answer/id #uuid "79028a7e-bbff-545e-9ea4-06f78d1324f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = ± \\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d041be2b-8164-55be-96b7-48c878a7e289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = ± (x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089607} :answer-field/id #uuid "300b7fc9-ed76-584d-9666-29645bda10bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b62a5af-9003-5857-84de-8df378771243" :question/math-academy-id "q-16443" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+8x+7=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+8x+7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x-{y}^{2}+7 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}-16-{y}^{2}+7 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}-{y}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(-4,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny=\\pm (x+4).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c96e6d6f-22be-5bfd-b24e-be9e34fcb149" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d801b43-9d32-5779-9c24-3e20300f86a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm 2(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340748} {:answer/id #uuid "109adc47-074c-5036-9bd9-7f8e12dd324f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340749} {:answer/id #uuid "141f4379-0020-535c-9a73-6d32b2c4a648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\pm 2(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340750} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccd7295-f60b-52a5-bca5-208b8f37a52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340751} {:answer/id #uuid "b664977d-3498-5110-b62b-29096d0986f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=\\pm \\frac{1}{4}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "109adc47-074c-5036-9bd9-7f8e12dd324f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340749} :answer-field/id #uuid "75099df9-a9a2-50ab-ad37-4000e1c51e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0100428-02c8-5814-8ee2-1f6e35bb4249" :question/math-academy-id "q-165470" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(2,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny+2=\\pm \\frac{2}{4}(x-2)\\,\\,y+2=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x-2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d19a514c-faa5-5f69-9870-87f78878f08a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1a034ce-810a-5912-9ad3-8dd4c55ee450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=\\pm \\frac{1}{4}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340755} {:answer/id #uuid "c084646b-ddf7-530d-ab8f-86171196bca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm 2(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340756} {:answer/id #uuid "53bef55b-4c75-54dc-a46e-b16219998816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340757} {:answer/id #uuid "dd995755-b973-55ab-b259-3b034c93df53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340758} {:answer/id #uuid "8d75ac35-239c-5e52-8565-2de81fd4e5aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm 4(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd995755-b973-55ab-b259-3b034c93df53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340758} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e32863-3b4e-53fc-b0ff-62f4d944d47a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d765260f-7408-51ca-af7e-de1bc557eb5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-165482" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-8y-4x-16=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-8y-4x-16 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-8y-{x}^{2}-4x-16 & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-2y]-[{x}^{2}+4x]-16 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y-1)^{2}-1]-[(x+2)^{2}-4]-16 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-4-(x+2)^{2}+4-16 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-(x+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{16}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(-2,1),\\,a=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c96e6d6f-22be-5bfd-b24e-be9e34fcb149" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3331a03e-7974-58a0-b50c-a033eee11aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3331a03e-7974-58a0-b50c-a033eee11aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340762} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3f1f941-0747-59c0-b4db-d88ce9133b49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96fcb93f-cc36-54fe-9086-2e51445cb005" :question/math-academy-id "q-325348" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $(x-7)^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at ((h,k)) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(7,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{1}=1,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{3}{1})(x-7) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,3(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c96e6d6f-22be-5bfd-b24e-be9e34fcb149" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b69da2-d663-5c1e-9614-b91c4385132d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26b69da2-d663-5c1e-9614-b91c4385132d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340765} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5ed917-dec8-5b00-a5d2-ede945656720" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8c36a82-4021-5469-908b-8de473f8be8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-325351" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(5,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{4}{3})(x-5) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,\\frac{4}{3}(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ace3c4b0-6b1c-595d-bc60-b89e2b24f6b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efde410c-eeb1-5310-b905-ba8a174a4fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efde410c-eeb1-5310-b905-ba8a174a4fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340768} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a21931-e8d3-5203-862d-5fd7dd016b51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb21eb3a-428b-5941-aee7-65dc2cddf525" :question/math-academy-id "q-325367" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{27}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{12}=1.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(4,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}})(x-4) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,\\frac{3}{2}(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56abbbc0-080d-5586-931f-957bd2b793b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b02cfe4b-b84a-5177-ba5f-a72d00e0a714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340771} {:answer/id #uuid "684b5f20-eab6-5da3-aa5b-f880a807bd47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "684b5f20-eab6-5da3-aa5b-f880a807bd47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340772} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2e259a-77d0-5ac7-8711-3f7aa387e98a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6231a16-b519-5567-8003-872b8131c4e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-325375" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-24x=0.$\n\n$y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Group the $x$ terms and complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-24x & =0 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-6x)-9{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4\\,[(x-3)^{2}-9]-9{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}-36-9{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{4(x-3)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus it is a horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nso the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\n(h,k)=(3,0),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\pm (\\frac{2}{3})(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\,\\frac{2}{3}(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d19a514c-faa5-5f69-9870-87f78878f08a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b28774d-9e6b-5d7f-9a67-3fc1e224e3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340775} {:answer/id #uuid "a0479e9b-ab8a-5fbb-89f2-8ae56cf236fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b28774d-9e6b-5d7f-9a67-3fc1e224e3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340775} :answer-field/id #uuid "b30cb763-a63f-5877-8a13-2109d112d867" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c8087f5-2d7c-54c8-b353-6ced9dbb25ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c8087f5-2d7c-54c8-b353-6ced9dbb25ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340778} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3bd35c-20e6-5fc9-bf8f-a8720c437373" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95fc4051-ef0b-536b-911f-79cd27270db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-325436" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}+54y-27=0.$\n\n$y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ terms and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}+54y-27 & =0 \\\\ 9({y}^{2}+6y)-4{x}^{2}-27 & =0 \\\\ 9\\,[(y+3)^{2}-9]-4{x}^{2}-27 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+3)^{2}-81-4{x}^{2}-27 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+3)^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ \\frac{9(y+3)^{2}}{108}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{108} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{27} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-3),\\,a=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-3) & =\\pm (\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3\\sqrt{3}})(x-0) \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d19a514c-faa5-5f69-9870-87f78878f08a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "957b593f-cf9b-5c8a-9f34-31be36bad502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "957b593f-cf9b-5c8a-9f34-31be36bad502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340781} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e16298-3a18-58c9-bfbf-87a322e4df24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afd8c2d3-a6ee-584a-95af-77d0e5c87362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afd8c2d3-a6ee-584a-95af-77d0e5c87362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340783} :answer-field/id #uuid "69efbad8-269c-5b98-9ae4-2b3ebb5255b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e46f8545-8a61-54bd-8997-8c67725dfcd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325439" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}+36y-16{x}^{2}+32x-124=0.$\n\n$y\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}+36y-16{x}^{2}+32x-124 & =0 \\\\ 9({y}^{2}+4y)-16({x}^{2}-2x)-124 & =0 \\\\ 9\\,[(y+2)^{2}-4]-16\\,[(x-1)^{2}-1]-124 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-36-16(x-1)^{2}+16-124 & =0 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-16(x-1)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{9(y+2)^{2}}{144}-\\frac{16(x-1)^{2}}{144} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(1,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =\\pm (\\frac{4}{3})(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\,\\frac{4}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56abbbc0-080d-5586-931f-957bd2b793b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7953033-81e7-51d6-8a33-55c75ed95a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 2 = ± \\frac{1}{4}(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091146} {:answer/id #uuid "441f2c19-d4d8-52c0-aae7-1e1942639a4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 1 = ± \\frac{1}{4}(x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091147} {:answer/id #uuid "45b0cbef-b34c-505a-9297-af32174ec48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 = ± 4(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091148} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0d1220-47b4-5f49-a7ca-290c29c259e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 4 = ± 2(x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091149} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1d93e6-afd5-5d65-8f6c-a6d0f60222ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 2 = (x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45b0cbef-b34c-505a-9297-af32174ec48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 = ± 4(x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091148} :answer-field/id #uuid "b74d1a67-339a-5ca1-a1f4-d7d17388eaae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99b70427-adca-5add-87bd-308032db0a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-83906" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $16{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-64x-2y+47=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-64x-2y+47 & =0 \\\\ (16{x}^{2}-64x)+(-{y}^{2}-2y)+47 & =0 \\\\ 16[{x}^{2}-4x]-[{y}^{2}+2y]+47 & =0 \\\\ 16[(x-2)^{2}-4]-[(y+1)^{2}-1]+47 & =0 \\\\ 16(x-2)^{2}-64-(y+1)^{2}+1+47 & =0 \\\\ 16(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =64-1-47 \\\\ 16(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{16(x-2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{16}{16} \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{1}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nh=2,\\,k=-1,\\,a=\\sqrt{1}=1,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{1}(x-2) \\\\  & =\\pm 4(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d19a514c-faa5-5f69-9870-87f78878f08a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c12a8fa-a26d-5309-892c-8e9a92622266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+4=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340786} {:answer/id #uuid "57cbbed7-408b-563c-b74b-e5f6445b8879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340787} {:answer/id #uuid "fa868989-7d98-5e92-a18a-4baead0eef1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340788} {:answer/id #uuid "20ce194f-2520-5d97-aae1-348472b69cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340789} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0da25e-32de-56a2-9bc2-28fe465bac18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20ce194f-2520-5d97-aae1-348472b69cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340789} :answer-field/id #uuid "5975b365-40ae-52c5-a408-39240e5e216c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0226369-319a-5694-ae58-86b4c1cede6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-83909" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}+54x-32y=53.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ terms and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}+54x-32y & =53 \\\\ (4{y}^{2}-32y)+(-9{x}^{2}+54x) & =53 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-8y]-9[{x}^{2}-6x] & =53 \\\\ 4[(y-4)^{2}-16]-9[(x-3)^{2}-9] & =53 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-64-9(x-3)^{2}+81 & =53 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-9(x-3)^{2} & =53+64-81 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-9(x-3)^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{4(y-4)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9(x-3)^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{36}{36} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(3,4),\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-4=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ace3c4b0-6b1c-595d-bc60-b89e2b24f6b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66e3ad4c-eb56-5174-a83c-f773d53f7ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340793} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1bc40b-4924-5398-8f22-16b145198859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=\\pm (x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340794} {:answer/id #uuid "041f5dcd-b417-59da-8c4b-a540f159c9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340795} {:answer/id #uuid "23cf9c0e-c549-5d1c-97af-cc1933ce51d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{4}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340796} {:answer/id #uuid "46c86fe9-25d0-5dc2-93ef-8c695aa4abcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "041f5dcd-b417-59da-8c4b-a540f159c9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340795} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5ebc15-0dbb-5f04-8d05-d61c6db5716f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1afa5e12-9529-5f81-952c-e590af0c972f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84071" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,h=0,\\,k=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny-1=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c96e6d6f-22be-5bfd-b24e-be9e34fcb149" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98940301-a111-5e22-9516-101749319a9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{2}{5}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340800} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfbe3ed-737b-5023-bcb2-7c8652973dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{2}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340801} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b3969e-d944-5756-b4c8-0f752ec5c6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340802} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8e50a8-80b8-53bc-a63b-daec18249b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{2}{5}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340803} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3d9d3e-e2bf-5e50-a323-e8c90b7e571d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cfbe3ed-737b-5023-bcb2-7c8652973dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{2}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340801} :answer-field/id #uuid "393e3e17-1d67-51ad-a6ed-85cf23561feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a30bba7-fc52-5f5d-90db-d3ba7b6cbacd" :question/math-academy-id "q-86650" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{25}=5,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny+2=\\pm \\frac{2}{5}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ace3c4b0-6b1c-595d-bc60-b89e2b24f6b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9ecf4d-891d-534d-be72-e61cc3c291fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340807} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e0e7b0-bde7-5b43-ac47-9660aae74b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+4=\\pm \\frac{1}{6}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340808} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3b6407-ccf9-532f-a9d1-dc5fb72c5c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\pm \\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340809} {:answer/id #uuid "e1642e93-fe83-5b8f-8d0c-780172697c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+6=\\pm \\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340810} {:answer/id #uuid "88614a54-b102-5614-bebd-aa95e292998e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3=\\pm \\frac{1}{6}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1642e93-fe83-5b8f-8d0c-780172697c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+6=\\pm \\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340810} :answer-field/id #uuid "4079636d-006c-59bc-b3e8-d2c8e837ac3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36f24a22-52a5-5e59-ae0a-c05243ab9298" :question/math-academy-id "q-86663" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{36}=6,\\,h=0,\\,k=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =\\pm (\\frac{2}{6})(x-0) \\\\ y+6 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56abbbc0-080d-5586-931f-957bd2b793b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fbf05f1-9bcc-592c-b81a-b7ce3f49de9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{3}{4}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340814} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5aa16b-b756-5ef4-8a46-7bc63618764e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340815} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea00e33-799b-569c-8e10-371909d3e064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340816} {:answer/id #uuid "31defa8c-960b-522e-8a1e-dbca0a770cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{4}{3}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340817} {:answer/id #uuid "85d9242b-7ef3-5c68-bb4e-7c42d3fbf484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ea00e33-799b-569c-8e10-371909d3e064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340816} :answer-field/id #uuid "509b81c5-0ba0-5c32-8ddd-3f7634159945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b4aa972-1824-5c7a-b4a4-7df673a2b3ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-86672" :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}+16x-20=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ terms and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}+16x-20 & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+16x)-9{y}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+4x]-9{y}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x+2)^{2}-4]-9{y}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 4(x+2)^{2}-16-9{y}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 4(x+2)^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =20+16 \\\\ 4(x+2)^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{4(x+2)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{36}{36} \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nh=-2,\\,k=0,\\,a=\\sqrt{9}=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
